        <h1>Recommended Mathematical Texts</h1>
        <p class="meta">James Squires • December 12, 2025</p>

        <p>Here are several texts that I'd love for more people to understand:</p>

        <section>
            <h2>Symmetry by Herman Weyl</h2>
            <p>This was recommended to me by one my beloved professors. The syntopical nature of the text makes it an S-tier read. Weyl explores various forms of symmetry through art, biology, and other phenomena. Honestly, the text was quite reminiscent of Jung's <em>Man and His Symbols</em> in terms of progression.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Man and His Symbols by Carl Jung</h2>
            <p>This text is nothing less than a paradigm shift. It has served me a great deal through means of self-reflection and perspective of the world. This type of thinking, in my opinion, is also a generator of creativity.</p>
            <p>It teaches you to think of ideas associativity; I don't know if this is true creativity, but it seems to produce similar results to what creativity would.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Elements by Euclid</h2>
            <p>The math text that keeps on giving. Ironically, I was recommended this by my Ancient Greek teacher. The text is a wonderful way to learn mathematics; the grammar is not baffling like that of Thucydides or Plato, the vocabulary is much more behaved than that of Homer. It honestly felt close to reading Mark or Xenophon.</p>
            <p>Enough of the Greek though—I'm recommending this for the math (the Greek is a bonus). What needs to be said? This is the equivalent of getting off your phone for a week and living in nature. The geometric thinking is quite difficult for someone who doesn't typically do ruler-and-compass this intensively, but it is also highly rewarding.</p>
            <p>I haven't thoroughly examined it, but I also think Euclid is quite constructive or even intuitionistic. His proof by contradiction that I read assumed not not P to prove not P—this is actually allowed even without Law of Excluded Middle. Lastly, the axiomatic formulation is quite beautiful. I suspect Euclid understood the notion of a primitive, but I think when he tried to define points and lines, he was drawing on real world descriptions rather than abstracting into a world of forms like we might do now.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Meditations by Marcus Aurelius and Enchiridion by Epictetus</h2>
            <p>I have a special love for the stoics. This is ironic considering my thorough rejection of stoicism; it was my gateway philosophy though. Meditations, Epictetus' Enchiridion, Seneca's letters, these were all very interesting. Stoicism is quite axiomatic actually, although I believe stoicism also demonstrates the risk of axiomatization without the αξιος—'worthiness'. We want to choose axioms that coincide with reality, it seems, at least for me, that stoic axioms do not coincide with what's best.</p>
            <p>Although I'm always open to reconsideration. I will note that Chryssipus was the O.G. father of propositional logic; having read these texts, this seems like a very believable statement. I'm personally a fan of the Hellenistic school of thought more than Roman stoicism. I quite like Epictetus for this reason. Even though he grew up in Rome, his teachings were undoubtedly Hellenistic in nature.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Republic by Plato</h2>
            <p>This might offend some people, but Aristotle and Plato can seem banal quite often—at least from a certain approach. If you read them seeking obvious novelty, this is a mistake. If you read something like Republic seeking to understand the memes that now inhabit everyone's mind around you... now that's interesting. Plato's works represent the epitome of western thought, for better or worse. I personally side with Thrasymachus though, which brings us to our next book....</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Beyond Good and Evil by Frederick Nietzsche</h2>
            <p>The most worthy followup! I really don't recommend this book unless you've first read at least two stoic books, two Plato books, a significant portion of the New Testament, at least some Kant, Schopenhauer (this can stand in for stoicism if it has to, they're all the same flavor really), and maybe you've also done a bit of philology.</p>
            <p>I know this seems quite pretentious, but I assure you, reading Nietzsche without having exhausted yourself with Christian—or even Platonist thought—is far more pretentious and silly in my eyes. A wrecking ball is useless in the wilderness. That being said, if you have read all of these, this is my first recommendation.</p>
            <p>Don't read his earlier works first, those are essentially a different person. Sometimes it's best to analyze things in a nonlinear fashion. Also, don't read Thus Spoke Zarathustra unless you've read this and maybe one of his other contemporaneous works. Once again, this book represents a paradigm shift. It is also a good concomitant to Jung's works, as Jung is essentially the offspring of Nietzsche. I'd argue that Jung often dichotomizes, or even Christianizes them, in a way that Nietzsche might find distasteful, although this would require more investigation for me to assert confidently.</p>
            <p>If you've truly read this book well, I'm already there's no going back, you'll have taken the red pill. I don't know if I even took the red pill. It seems to me Nietzsche is often whitewashed or Christianized. His ideas aren't just that of rejection of platonism and christianity as a governing doctrine, they're not just a rejection of contemporary morality and ethics, they are an upheaval of everything that you know. This even means a rejection of logic itself; this is perfectly valid when one does not bound themselves by the logos, but rather the will to power.</p>
            <p>I had to reread this book twice in order to understand this. The first time I was quite confused, as I was looking for philosophical justification—this is not what Nietzsche does though. The structure of his arguments themselves—assertions by his own volition, allude to this understanding. It's what lies between the lines that truly makes this book worth reading. Ever since having touched Nietzsche's works, I've found a constant tension between philosophies; I have to hold many worlds in my head simultaneously. In my opinion, this parallels the many worlds, or models, in logic. Which brings me to my next recommendations.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Five Stages of Accepting Constructive Mathematics</h2>
            <p><a href="https://www.ams.org/journals/bull/2017-54-03/S0273-0979-2016-01556-4/S0273-0979-2016-01556-4.pdf" class="text-link">The mathematical red pill.</a> Will you view this world through algorithm and computation... or dichotomy that isomorphically resembles the good that Plato imbued all forms with; just as propositions have truth values in classical logic, all forms are underpinned with αγαθα.</p>
            <p>A professor that I talked to suggested intuitionism is Kantian or even Hegelian in nature. I haven't read enough to affirm or deny this claim. I will say that, even though I believe Nietzsche himself would not say this, constructivism seems a very Nietzschean flavored mathematics to me.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Type Theory and Formal Proof: An Introduction by Geuvers and Nederpelt</h2>
            <p>And if you want an elegant constructive model, look no further than type theory. I'm very fond of this book, it was quite enjoyable to read. There are many forms of logic, even constructivism, that I recommend. I think this one and/or the BHK interpretation are the best to start with though. Homotopy type theory is a bit beyond me to recommend. And I'm afraid if you are an ultra finitist, you might be ostracized like you have the plague.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Lord of the Rings by J. R. R. Tolkien</h2>
            <p>I will not justify this addition. I will say that this book is extraordinarily interesting when you scrutinize it through a Jungian lens.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>The Dresden Codex</h2>
            <p>Only in Mayan! Who knows what Mayan glyphs will be discovered in 50 years, but I'm invested. If you're a true perspectivist, Nietzsche isn't good enough. I'd argue you need to read Sumerian or Mayan. And once again, not to sound like a broken record, but an analysis of the very nature of this language is quite interesting from a Jungian perspective.</p>
            <p>I'm still making my way through the Codex, but the Mayan perspective on time, and also the intertwined relation between science and theology; I believe it seems to evidence an introspective and mature acceptance of the mythos we all reside in.</p>
        </section>
